146	// Herdr runs the status command on a fixed timer and does *not* re-run it when
147	// focus changes, so this interval is the worst-case lag after switching tabs or
148	// workspaces. The command is a bare `type`/`cat` of the cache file the resident
149	// daemon keeps fresh (lib/tabline.js) — Herdr used to boot a whole node here
150	// every interval, which cost more CPU than everything the line ever showed.
151	// Herdr takes the last line of output; a dead daemon leaves a stale line, and
152	// a missing file makes the command fail, which hides the segment entirely.
153	//
154	// Six seconds, not two. A path that costs a process on Windows should not be
155	// walked three times as often to shave four seconds off a line that only says
156	// which directory you are in — and the cost is paid on every tick whether or
157	// not anything changed, since there is no way to ask Herdr to re-run this on
158	// focus. Herdr's tab bar has no file source: `TabBarRightEntryConfig` is
159	// Zoom / Hostname / Time / Text / Command, so a command is the only way in.
160	// 🔴 The timeout must stay BELOW the interval, and it is derived here so it
161	// cannot drift apart from it again.
162	//
163	// It did once, and the machine it happened on became unusable: the interval was
164	// 2s against a 3s timeout, so a command that ran long was still running when the
165	// next one started. On Windows every one of these is a fresh `cmd.exe` — Herdr's
166	// own config says so — and the overlap feeds itself: more overlap, slower
167	// machine, more timeouts, more overlap. It ended at 12.5 cores of Herdr, 35
168	// console hosts, a hundred shells, and a mouse that would not move. Six seconds
169	// against two leaves four seconds of slack on a file read that takes
170	// milliseconds, and the derivation keeps a future edit of one from silently
171	// inverting the pair.
